{{template "head" (dict "Title" "Flugschreiber: tamper-evident audit logs for self-hosted LLMs" "Repo" .Repo)}}

<main id="main">

<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">EVIDENCE LAYER FOR SELF-HOSTED LLMS</p>
      <h1>Prove what your AI did, and that nobody changed the record.</h1>
      <p class="lede">A reverse proxy in front of any OpenAI-compatible endpoint. It
      records every model interaction to an append-only, hash-chained, signed log,
      and generates the EU AI Act documentation from the traffic it observed. One
      base URL change, no application code.</p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#quickstart">Sixty-second start</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="{{.Repo}}">View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="demo-frame">
      <div class="chrome"><span></span><span></span><span></span><b>serve &middot; call &middot; verify &middot; tamper &middot; report</b></div>
      <img src="media/demo.gif" width="842" height="612"
           alt="A terminal recording: starting the proxy, making a model call, verifying the chain, tampering with a record, and watching verification fail.">
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="claim">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p>Somebody will ask what evidence you have of what your AI did last quarter.
    Application logs were never designed to answer that.</p>
    <p class="after">Flugschreiber records the interaction, chains it so a change
    is detectable, signs it so a rewrite needs the key, and hands you documentation
    built from what actually happened.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="caps">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <div class="cap-grid">
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Records everything</h3>
        <p class="cmd">serve</p>
        <p>Chat, completions, embeddings and the Responses API, streamed or not.
        Tool calls and their results. Three content modes, from a digest of every
        request to full transcripts, with encryption at rest.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Proves it is intact</h3>
        <p class="cmd">verify</p>
        <p>Every record carries the hash of the one before it. Ed25519 checkpoints,
        chained to each other, mean a rewrite needs the signing key and a deletion
        is detectable. <code>verify</code> reads only the files, on any machine.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Keeps the key elsewhere</h3>
        <p class="cmd">keys &middot; --signer</p>
        <p>An external signer holds the key on a smartcard or an HSM. RFC 3161
        anchoring turns a checkpoint's time into a third party's claim. Key rotation
        keeps every retired key, so old checkpoints verify forever.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Answers erasure requests</h3>
        <p class="cmd">erase</p>
        <p>Stored content is sealed under a per-session key held outside the chain.
        Erasing destroys the key, so the content goes and the chain stays whole. The
        log records what was erased and when.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Generates the documents</h3>
        <p class="cmd">report</p>
        <p>The Annex IV technical documentation skeleton and the Article 50
        transparency pack, in English and German, pre-filled from observed traffic
        as Markdown, HTML and PDF.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Runs where you run</h3>
        <p class="cmd">helm &middot; docker</p>
        <p>A 20&nbsp;MB distroless binary with zero dependencies. A Helm chart with a
        network policy and scheduled verification. No SaaS, no dashboard to host, no
        telemetry of any kind.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="quickstart" id="quickstart">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="lead">
      <h2>Sixty seconds</h2>
      <p>Point a container at your model server, send a request, and verify the
      chain. No GPU and no network needed: the built-in mock stands in for a real
      upstream.</p>
      <p><a href="kubernetes.html">The Kubernetes guide</a> covers the chart, the
      sidecar mode and the network policy that makes the proxy unavoidable.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal">
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="c">docker run</span> -d --name flugschreiber -p 8080:8080 \
    -v fs-evidence:/var/lib/flugschreiber \
    ghcr.io/ramazankara/flugschreiber \
    serve --mock-upstream
<span class="o"></span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="c">curl</span> localhost:8080/v1/chat/completions \
    -d '{"model":"m","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hi"}]}'
<span class="o"></span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="c">docker exec</span> flugschreiber \
    flugschreiber verify --dir /var/lib/flugschreiber
<span class="o">hash chain intact</span>
<span class="o">  records     1</span>
<span class="o">  checkpoints 1, 1 verified against signature and chain</span>
<span class="o">  attestation attested</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="docs">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Documentation</h2>
    {{range .Groups}}
    <div class="doc-group">
      <h3>{{.Name}}</h3>
      <div class="doc-list">
        {{range .Pages}}
        <a class="doc-item" href="{{.Slug}}.html">
          <span class="t">{{.Title}}</span>
          <p class="d">{{.Blurb}}</p>
        </a>
        {{end}}
      </div>
    </div>
    {{end}}
  </div>
</section>

</main>

{{template "foot" (dict "Version" .Version "Repo" .Repo)}}
